/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A defect is physical: a metal short, an open contact, a missing implant, a pinhole in thin oxide.
   - A defect has a size, a resistance and a place, and its effect changes with all three.
   - A line is a primary input, a primary output, a gate input or a gate output; each branch of a fanout is a separate line.
   - where N is the number of single stuck-at faults and L the number of lines; the 2 counts SA0 and SA1.
   - Two faults are equivalent if no input vector can tell their faulty circuits apart.
   - Fault f dominates fault g if every test for g also detects f.
   - For a whole circuit the checkpoint theorem says a test set that detects every stuck-at fault on the primary inputs and on the fanout branches detects every single stuck-at fault in the circuit.
   - A transistor stuck-open never conducts; a transistor stuck-short always conducts.
   - A bridging fault shorts two lines.
   - A transition delay fault makes one line slow-to-rise or slow-to-fall.
*/
