/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Fault simulation applies each vector to the good circuit and to every faulty circuit in the collapsed fault list.
   - A large chip has millions of faults, so speed matters.
   - where FC is the fault coverage, 'detected' is the number of faults the vectors detect and 'total faults' is the size of the collapsed fault list.
   - Tools also report fault efficiency (some call it ATPG efficiency or test coverage): detected faults divided by the faults left after removing those ATPG proved redundant.
   - Exhaustive test applies all 2ⁿ vectors of an n-input circuit: complete, but impossible beyond small n.
   - In the D-algorithm, D means 1 in the good circuit and 0 in the faulty circuit; D̄ means 0 in the good and 1 in the faulty circuit.
   - PODEM assigns values only at primary inputs, chosen by tracing back from the current objective, so its search space is the 2ⁿ input combinations rather than all internal lines and it usually backtracks less.
   - A fault with no test is undetectable.
   - Test compaction removes vectors that detect nothing new and merges vectors whose specified bits do not clash.
   - The loop in the figure is iterative: simulate, check coverage, run ATPG on the undetected faults, add the new vectors, simulate again.
*/
